# podium — the one slab the whole building stands on: plinth, rusticated die, cornice, terrace.
#
# Top at y = 1.80, the main floor. ONE slab, x -16.65..16.65 and z -13.05..8.10, so that the
# portico, both wings, the main block and the head of the great steps all land on the same piece
# of stone. An earlier facility had three slabs with the stair hanging over the gap between them;
# there is no gap here to hang over.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE PUTS UNDER LOAD — two things, and they pull in opposite directions on purpose.
#
# 1. THE LARGEST SOLID MASS IN THE BUILDING. 33.30 x 21.15 x 2.25 m of stone, about 1560 cubic
#    metres, roughly 51 million voxels at the contract's metre 32. Almost all of it is interior:
#    one material, no variation reachable, nothing to look at. That is the point. It is the
#    storage layer's cheapest case — the uniform brick — and it is the only thing in this scene
#    that produces it in bulk. If uniform bricks ever stop being uniform, or stop being cheap,
#    this part is where the memory figure will say so first, and it will say so loudly.
#
#    THEREFORE THIS PART IS NOT WEATHERED, and that is deliberate, not an oversight. `weather`
#    displaces and recolours by geometry, and coating the largest mass in the building would turn
#    the one clean uniform-storage case in the scene into millions of per-voxel exceptions. The
#    walls, the steps and the site are outdoor stone too and are better placed to carry the
#    weathering share. If you want the podium weathered, scope it to `podium_zone_base` — the
#    bottom 0.45 m only — and leave the mass alone.
#
# 2. DEEP NARROW GROOVES, about 1200 metres of them, cut into that mass.
#
#      rustication joints   0.045 m wide (M/10) and 0.090 m deep (M/5)
#      terrace paving joints 0.0375 m wide (M/12) and 0.045 m deep (M/10)
#
#    At metre 32 a voxel is 0.03125 m. So a rustication channel is ONE AND A HALF voxels across
#    and just under three deep, and a paving joint is ONE AND HALF A TENTH across and one and a
#    half deep. A groove is the hardest case of thin matter there is: unlike a fin, it is a
#    surface that folds back on itself inside a single sample step, so a sampler that steps by its
#    reported distance can walk straight over the mouth of one and never know it was there. If the
#    contact sheet shows the rustication as a smudge or the paving as nothing at all, the SAMPLER
#    is what is wrong, not the grooves.
#
#    DO NOT WIDEN THEM. 0.045 and 0.0375 are M/10 and M/12; they are module fractions and they are
#    at the limit on purpose. A podium with comfortable 0.10 m channels tests nothing.
#
# The third thing, which is free: the mitres. The whole block is the INTERSECTION of two extruded
# profiles, one drawn in (x, y) and run along z, one drawn in (z, y) and run along x. That is what
# makes every moulding turn the four corners as a true mitre rather than as the fat square corner
# a union of four straight runs gives you. It also means the field is an intersection of two
# ten-band unions over fifty million voxels, which is a fair test of box culling on its own.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MODULE. M = 0.45, D = 0.90. Every number below is a multiple of M or of M/12 = 0.0375:
#
#   16.65 = 37 M     16.425 = 36.5 M     16.20 = 36 M          the three faces, east and west
#   13.05 = 29 M     12.825 = 28.5 M     12.60 = 28 M          the same three, south
#    8.10 = 18 M      7.875 = 17.5 M      7.65 = 17 M          the same three, north
#
# The main block is x -16.00..16.00 and z -7.50..7.50 by the contract and those are not module
# numbers, so the terrace ledge round it comes out 0.65 at the sides, 0.60 north and 0.55 south of
# the portico front. The podium is dimensioned by the module and the block sits on it; the other
# way round would have put a number in this file that nothing could be derived from.
#
# WHERE YOUR PART MEETS MINE
#
#   the terrace floor        y = 1.80 everywhere on top. Stand anything on it and sink it 0.05.
#   the south face           z = -13.05 (plinth and corona), z = -12.60 (the rusticated die)
#   the north face           z =  8.10 / 7.65
#   the east and west faces  x = +-16.65 / +-16.20
#   the underside            y = -0.45, buried, for the site to bring its ground up to.
#
#   THE GREAT STEPS: the top tread must land flush at y = 1.80 on or north of z = -13.05. Ten
#   risers of 0.18 and ten runs of 0.32 is 3.20 m, so the flight runs z = -16.25 (ground) to
#   z = -13.05 (top nosing). That is 0.55 m further south than the contract's steps zone; the
#   alternative is that the last two risers are buried in this slab and the stair arrives at a
#   0.36 m wall. The slab is 0.55 m deeper than the contract asked for because the brief for this
#   part says it must reach z = -13, and it does.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE SECTION — the same profile on all four sides, 1.80 above ground and 0.45 below
# ================================================================================================
#
#   -0.4500 .. 0.0000   footing, buried, at the plinth face          M
#    0.0000 .. 0.2250   plinth                                       M/2
#    0.2250 .. 0.3375   ovolo, plinth face in to the middle face     M/4
#    0.3375 .. 0.4500   cyma, middle face in to the die face         M/4
#    0.4500 .. 1.3500   the die — rusticated, two courses of 0.45    2M
#    1.3500 .. 1.4625   ovolo bed mould, die face out to the middle  M/4
#    1.4625 .. 1.5000   fillet                                       M/12
#    1.5000 .. 1.8000   corona, back out to the plinth face          2M/3
#
# Sum above ground: 0.225 + 0.1125 + 0.1125 + 0.90 + 0.1125 + 0.0375 + 0.30 = 1.80 exactly.
#
# The corona overhangs the fillet by 0.225 with a flat soffit, which is where the black line under
# the top of a podium comes from; the plinth and the corona are flush at 16.65 and the die is
# recessed 0.45 between them, which is what makes the rusticated band read as sunk rather than as
# a change of colour.
#
# THE BASE IS TWO MOULDINGS AND NOT ONE, and that is the whole of what the first three contact
# sheets were for. The plinth stands 0.45 proud of the die, and the first attempt took all of it
# back in one 0.1125-high band — four across for one up. A moulding that flat is a shelf: the
# curve lies down so nearly flat that voxels turn it into four terraces, and it read on the sheet
# as exactly the stack of cylinders the brief tells you not to build. Split into an ovolo of
# 0.225 and a cyma of 0.225, each two across for one up — the same proportion as the bed mould
# above, which was the one that looked right — and it reads as carved. Do not merge them back.
#
# A moulding is only the band between its two corners and the stone behind it is mine, so the die
# box below runs from 0.3375 up to 1.4625 — through both moulding bands — and each moulding sits
# outside it. FIRST CORNER IN THE STONE, SECOND IN THE AIR: that is the whole of the orientation,
# and it is why the bed mould reads (die face, top) then (fillet face, bottom).
#
# `run=` IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE. It defaults to z, and with the default a moulding written along x
# has its section drawn across the full 33 m of the run and its curve carved in the wrong plane —
# it still fills its box, so it still looks like a fillet, and nothing tells you.

# --- the profile drawn in (x, y), run along the full depth ------------------------------------

let podium_plinth   = box -16.65 -0.45 -13.05   16.65 0.225 8.10 round=0.015
let podium_corona   = box -16.65  1.50 -13.05   16.65 1.80  8.10 round=0.018

let podium_x_low    = box -16.425 0.225  -13.05  16.425 0.3375 8.10 round=0.010
let podium_x_die    = box -16.20  0.3375 -13.05  16.20  1.4625 8.10 round=0.012
let podium_x_fillet = box -16.425 1.4625 -13.05  16.425 1.50   8.10 round=0.010
let podium_x_wash_e = ovolo  16.425 0.225  -13.05   16.65  0.3375 8.10 run=z
let podium_x_wash_w = ovolo -16.425 0.225  -13.05  -16.65  0.3375 8.10 run=z
let podium_x_cyma_e = cyma   16.20  0.3375 -13.05   16.425 0.45   8.10 run=z
let podium_x_cyma_w = cyma  -16.20  0.3375 -13.05  -16.425 0.45   8.10 run=z
let podium_x_bed_e  = ovolo  16.20  1.4625 -13.05   16.425 1.35   8.10 run=z
let podium_x_bed_w  = ovolo -16.20  1.4625 -13.05  -16.425 1.35   8.10 run=z

# THE ORDER OF A UNION IS A SPEED, not a taste. A union evaluates its children in turn and skips
# any whose bounding box is further away than the answer it already has, so the three bands that
# carry nearly all the surface — corona, die, plinth — go first and the eight small mouldings go
# last. Written bottom-to-top, as the section reads, this same profile cost 33.6 million field
# evaluations at metre 16; written biggest-first it costs about half that, because a voxel of the
# sky above the terrace now meets the corona on the first try instead of on the eleventh.
let podium_profile_x = union {
    podium_corona
    podium_x_die
    podium_plinth
    podium_x_low
    podium_x_fillet
    podium_x_wash_e podium_x_wash_w
    podium_x_cyma_e podium_x_cyma_w
    podium_x_bed_e  podium_x_bed_w
}

# --- the same profile drawn in (z, y), run along the full width --------------------------------

let podium_z_low    = box -16.65 0.225  -12.825  16.65 0.3375 7.875 round=0.010
let podium_z_die    = box -16.65 0.3375 -12.60   16.65 1.4625 7.65  round=0.012
let podium_z_fillet = box -16.65 1.4625 -12.825  16.65 1.50   7.875 round=0.010
let podium_z_wash_s = ovolo -16.65 0.225  -12.825  16.65 0.3375 -13.05 run=x
let podium_z_wash_n = ovolo -16.65 0.225    7.875  16.65 0.3375  8.10  run=x
let podium_z_cyma_s = cyma  -16.65 0.3375 -12.60   16.65 0.45   -12.825 run=x
let podium_z_cyma_n = cyma  -16.65 0.3375   7.65   16.65 0.45     7.875 run=x
let podium_z_bed_s  = ovolo -16.65 1.4625 -12.60   16.65 1.35   -12.825 run=x
let podium_z_bed_n  = ovolo -16.65 1.4625   7.65   16.65 1.35     7.875 run=x

let podium_profile_z = union {
    podium_corona
    podium_z_die
    podium_plinth
    podium_z_low
    podium_z_fillet
    podium_z_wash_s podium_z_wash_n
    podium_z_cyma_s podium_z_cyma_n
    podium_z_bed_s  podium_z_bed_n
}

# The mitre. Both profiles are solid right across the middle, so away from an edge the
# intersection is just the other one's profile; at a corner it is the smaller of the two, which is
# exactly what a mitred return is.
let podium_mass = intersection { podium_profile_x podium_profile_z }

# ================================================================================================
# THE RUSTICATION — 0.045 wide, 0.090 deep, two courses of 0.45, laid in running bond
# ================================================================================================
#
# The die is 0.90 tall, so two courses of M; the blocks are 1.35 long (3 M), three to one, and the
# die is a whole number of them BOTH WAYS by construction:
#
#   32.40 across  =  24 blocks of 1.35        20.25 deep  =  15 blocks of 1.35
#
# which is why the die is 36 M half-width and 28 M / 17 M in z rather than anything nearer the
# walls it carries. The joints therefore land exactly on all four arrises and there is no half
# block at a corner in the lower course; the upper course is offset half a block, so it has a
# half block at each corner, which is what running bond is.
#
# The channel is one slab repeated along its face and then cut down to a face zone, so one repeat
# serves the south face and the north face together. The joint slabs are limited repeats of a
# child far smaller than the period, which is exact and carries a box; nothing here needs
# `around`, and see clips/facility/requests/order.md for what `around` would cost.

let podium_joint_x   = box -0.0225 -1 -20   0.0225 3 20
let podium_joint_z   = box -20 -1 -0.0225   20 3 0.0225

# Lower course: joints every 1.35 from the centre line, out to 14.85, leaving a full block to the
# corner at 16.20. Upper course: the same, moved half a block, mirrored so both halves agree.
let podium_vj_low_x  = repeat { podium_joint_x } x=1.35 nx=11
let podium_vj_up_x   = mirror { translate { repeat { podium_joint_x } x=1.35 nx=11 } 0.675 0 0 } axis=x

# In z the die runs -12.60..7.65, so the joints sit at 0.90 + 1.35k; the two that would land on
# the corner arrises are cut off by the face zone below.
let podium_vj_low_z  = translate { repeat { podium_joint_z } z=1.35 nz=10 } 0 0 0.90
let podium_vj_up_z   = translate { repeat { podium_joint_z } z=1.35 nz=10 } 0 0 1.575

# The face zones: 0.09 into the stone and 0.05 out into the air, so the channel has a mouth even
# after the manifest's 12 mm of grain has pushed the surface outward.
let podium_face_s_low = box -16.70 0.45   -12.65  16.70 0.9225 -12.51
let podium_face_n_low = box -16.70 0.45     7.56  16.70 0.9225   7.70
let podium_face_s_up  = box -16.70 0.8775 -12.65  16.70 1.35   -12.51
let podium_face_n_up  = box -16.70 0.8775   7.56  16.70 1.35     7.70
let podium_face_e_low = mirror { box 16.11 0.45   -12.555  16.25 0.9225 7.605 } axis=x
let podium_face_e_up  = mirror { box 16.11 0.8775 -12.555  16.25 1.35   7.605 } axis=x

let podium_rust_v_low = intersection { podium_vj_low_x union { podium_face_s_low podium_face_n_low } }
let podium_rust_v_up  = intersection { podium_vj_up_x  union { podium_face_s_up  podium_face_n_up  } }
let podium_rust_v_elo = intersection { podium_vj_low_z podium_face_e_low }
let podium_rust_v_eup = intersection { podium_vj_up_z  podium_face_e_up  }

# The one horizontal channel, at 0.90, right round all four faces and round both corners.
let podium_rust_h_s  = box -16.70 0.8775 -12.65  16.70 0.9225 -12.51
let podium_rust_h_n  = box -16.70 0.8775   7.56  16.70 0.9225   7.70
let podium_rust_h_e  = mirror { box 16.11 0.8775 -12.70  16.25 0.9225 7.75 } axis=x

let podium_rustication = union {
    podium_rust_h_s podium_rust_h_n podium_rust_h_e
    podium_rust_v_low podium_rust_v_up
    podium_rust_v_elo podium_rust_v_eup
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE TERRACE PAVING — 1.35 m slabs, joints 0.0375 wide and 0.045 deep
# ================================================================================================
#
# The whole top is paved, not just the part outside the walls, because the part outside the walls
# is not knowable from here and a joint under a wall costs nothing and is never seen.
#
# The grid is offset half a slab from the centre line in both directions, so that the six portico
# columns — whose axes are at 1.35, 4.05 and 6.75, all odd multiples of the slab — each stand in
# the middle of a slab rather than astride a joint. It leaves a plain margin course 1.125 (2.5 M)
# wide all the way round, over the cornice, which is what a terrace edge wants.
let podium_pave_x = box -0.01875 1.755 -20   0.01875 1.82 20
let podium_pave_z = box -20 1.755 -0.01875   20 1.82 0.01875

let podium_paving = union {
    mirror { translate { repeat { podium_pave_x } x=1.35 nx=11 } 0.675 0 0 } axis=x
    translate { repeat { podium_pave_z } z=1.35 nz=10 } 0 0 1.575
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================

let podium_stone = difference { podium_mass podium_rustication podium_paving }

let part_podium = podium_stone

# Nothing is hollowed out of the podium — it is solid stone all the way through, which is the
# whole of its first job. There is no `void_podium` and the manifest does not ask for one.

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Every rule is keyed on a zone INTERSECTED WITH MY OWN STONE, so no rule can reach a voxel that
# belongs to somebody else even where their geometry passes through the same coordinates. And
# every rule is below=0.02, not below=0, because the manifest displaces the whole building by
# 12 mm at the end and a test at zero leaves a rind of the base coat over every outward face.
#
# A dark hard base, a pale cornice, a white floor: granite below, limestone at the top of the
# cornice, marble where it is walked. Three materials on one piece of stone, meeting along lines
# that are horizontal to within a voxel, which is a fair test of whether a paint rule's boundary
# is where its shape says it is.

# Everything from the footing to the top of the die is ONE material and one zone, on purpose: it
# is 1440 of the 1530 cubic metres in this part, and it is the uniform-brick case the first note
# at the top of this file is about. Splitting it into two coats for the sake of variety would cost
# the scene the only large uniform volume it has.
let podium_zone_base    = intersection { podium_stone  box -17 -0.50 -13.10  17 1.3500 8.15 }
let podium_zone_cornice = intersection { podium_stone  box -17  1.3500 -13.10  17 1.9000 8.15 }
let podium_zone_terrace = intersection { podium_stone  box -16.20 1.6875 -12.60  16.20 1.9000 7.65 }

paint granite   where=podium_zone_base    below=0.02
paint limestone where=podium_zone_cornice below=0.02
paint marble    where=podium_zone_terrace below=0.02
